/math-academy-id "q-44337" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-44337/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-44337"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "3b875168-aac2-5c76-b7c3-e98d96a73bac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection/0f5647700b933a18d4c924b388250a52d426eab8923c02afed2168e950a0d65a" :answer/id #uuid "e1a298b8-01e3-52e6-a1eb-3bcc573a85fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "553b9bed-025c-5ece-9d2d-ebc6843480b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "ef4f76b7-128b-5c43-a55f-a6482a9af0fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c63c6dfc-36e4-5c7b-ac97-e9492879f707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "02ead9df-d3a4-5fb9-a565-bbf300cf97b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-44606/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-44606/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-44606/selection/0f5647700b933a18d4c924b388250a52d426eab8923c02afed2168e950a0d65a" "q-44606/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-44606/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-44606/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-44606" :question/id #uuid "8dccbd68-1234-5f4e-b21e-948f34cd05c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-44606" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. Given that the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(-1,5),$ what is the value of $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-2$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y-5 & =-2x-2 \\\\ y & =-2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-44606/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-44606"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-237193/field-1/bbe8aa069fc00832f96dda718dd7061e8789ed9238fc818e82b92700f33f2763" :answer/id #uuid "1299bd78-18f8-50fb-8baf-2c768dace883" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237193/field-1/c40255a6865b942e7d80d54ee6ba6e6bff118ab7e4828a35bb066d02fadf2538" :answer/id #uuid "2cbc16bf-8e6b-5824-a7d5-7a0dec6c3cc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237193/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a66b41c-db0d-5cca-912a-80b5be4da9fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-237193/field-1/bbe8aa069fc00832f96dda718dd7061e8789ed9238fc818e82b92700f33f2763" :answer-field/choices ["q-237193/field-1/bbe8aa069fc00832f96dda718dd7061e8789ed9238fc818e82b92700f33f2763" "q-237193/field-1/c40255a6865b942e7d80d54ee6ba6e6bff118ab7e4828a35bb066d02fadf2538"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-237193" :question/id #uuid "7d7b950d-4468-5f50-9499-9d01afa18ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-237193" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/5add9f877dc2e93b1c8e562794aa64c83d180f5c683735055ee24f8a190e8740.png)\n\nThe equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=-4x-3$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$  What is the equation of ${l}_{2}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $-4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-4) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-237193/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237193"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-237197/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "1813979a-422c-5365-beae-dc322fe363db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237197/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0827810c-2459-54cc-bf9f-a6afaf30eccd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-237197/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer-field/choices ["q-237197/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-237197" :question/id #uuid "3e4defe2-68c2-5032-be33-7cb859509d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-237197" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. If the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(6,-1),$ what is the value of $b?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{2}{5},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(6,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=14.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-237197/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237197"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72567/selection/a53cf443fc14d4310482543d8b309579a0f2051581e7e85ddf3e33b217e107d8" :answer/id #uuid "f4046e3e-1549-522e-95c9-1df51a0eb033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72567/selection/a1cfaeae13a5b7a24e865794b43303ec1a11a0730eecd0c3fe8e9673c285ca04" :answer/id #uuid "1e065ea5-b657-55dd-bf38-765441fce976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72567/selection/5f8efbb1fec042c99afc7d6eef2c25b352126f8179aee23ce0cc5efeddfbf864" :answer/id #uuid "6bec70c9-5e65-5511-8369-e98b7f037611" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{6}x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72567/selection/c873766660f5a7497ed8079c8b9ee5b7d307b94cd6c49822630bca4acacae231" :answer/id #uuid "03483045-a4e7-57d8-a807-f45aeba59056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72567/selection/cb0b0681bb2675da0ad82d5bee2301aefc7068042c20ba52179b877370ee3c0d" :answer/id #uuid "a3aa901a-66ed-510b-9799-74d42822b0a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-