ndicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103455/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103455"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-237224/field-1/4a529c895211386a988bddf6c7e2165689977fc836d3f4c0de4ec98cc1dff15f" :answer/id #uuid "57488bd3-83d5-5db6-94fd-b8a126d276a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237224/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "23819259-60f2-55cc-98b9-8bbc4ba2b535" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-237224/field-1/4a529c895211386a988bddf6c7e2165689977fc836d3f4c0de4ec98cc1dff15f" :answer-field/choices ["q-237224/field-1/4a529c895211386a988bddf6c7e2165689977fc836d3f4c0de4ec98cc1dff15f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-237224" :question/id #uuid "d3e3189e-08d0-518b-8225-73f15324974f" :question/math-academy-id "q-237224" :question/is-example false :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-237224/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237224"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection/d5a16e7d29e99000f608fa75d967997688bd38702909b8362b99b93e08122009" :answer/id #uuid "fb1e04fa-e872-5646-ad5d-17579f4684f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2y=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection/f40ff764df531635bbcbe0a4b002c3e252826d42070ce4ca01ea72f5b32961c9" :answer/id #uuid "850e5bba-4731-5599-b16e-7a8c7a7e037d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection/3bf570f7e5006dfb39c9a2c0b8566cac6bbd3cecad56bf06bd7287741234393e" :answer/id #uuid "10d61639-c435-562a-97a3-b12c12a64b3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-2y=-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection/5dfe821d962d9e4d96850cbd3ea91be1d521015b1f5519c944c47c282f2471cd" :answer/id #uuid "fd636abb-fd4a-5e3b-be7b-2a1101756255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection/c100969fdb6b94667b19830468b87ac6298e6c5675be8cd118a29837b0e45e45" :answer/id #uuid "2ae18454-d341-5cc7-a2fd-d2f7a037e52b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+y=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e38ef1a-4ee3-531d-9211-cd1bb4399c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-103449/selection/f40ff764df531635bbcbe0a4b002c3e252826d42070ce4ca01ea72f5b32961c9" :answer-field/choices ["q-103449/selection/d5a16e7d29e99000f608fa75d967997688bd38702909b8362b99b93e08122009" "q-103449/selection/f40ff764df531635bbcbe0a4b002c3e252826d42070ce4ca01ea72f5b32961c9" "q-103449/selection/3bf570f7e5006dfb39c9a2c0b8566cac6bbd3cecad56bf06bd7287741234393e" "q-103449/selection/5dfe821d962d9e4d96850cbd3ea91be1d521015b1f5519c944c47c282f2471cd" "q-103449/selection/c100969fdb6b94667b19830468b87ac6298e6c5675be8cd118a29837b0e45e45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103449" :question/id #uuid "a074bb7c-f733-505c-a777-082de91b8ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103449" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103449/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103449"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection/1ee8f99df34ed786cf4522488e1eb11ce075614a56b85a6786112de2fcf5cb0d" :answer/id #uuid "a920e7b8-ecbd-5444-a4b7-a9bf8fdfc6ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection/c912e991254af6de3f7f02b324be275c13e9ff90e0ebebeca61af5db48d65c8a" :answer/id #uuid "ed224373-3e75-571a-8299-ea9867c56239" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-y=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection/63e75be0fcccd9265eea93dc1eb38765875c7b7cadef0cb5683a25232c1af25c" :answer/id #uuid "605d03d7-51e9-53a3-887a-8482ecc9319e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection/99a57b462b30a705b7102a9150e506f8f71007a5ab8d55c364ec005f61729349" :answer/id #uuid "097d4e64-f405-57b6-bd5b-957c47f6e9d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+4y=19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection/0c27ba7b8e7e3262515046802ab5d7dda4fc4a2ee0901fa0b1772b0b9d67bc24" :answer/id #uuid "81266fc1-fe0c-51e1-9374-4ba7b1deca75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+5y=26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4e7f27-bcce-5fe9-a537-b648f9f75292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-252524/selection/1ee8f99df34ed786cf4522488e1eb11ce075614a56b85a6786112de2fcf5cb0d" :answer-field/choices ["q-252524/selection/1ee8f99df34ed786cf4522488e1eb11ce075614a56b85a6786112de2fcf5cb0d" "q-252524/selection/c912e991254af6de3f7f02b324be275c13e9ff90e0ebebeca61af5db48d65c8a" "q-252524/selection/63e75be0fcccd9265eea93dc1eb38765875c7b7cadef0cb5683a25232c1af25c" "q-252524/selection/99a57b462b30a705b7102a9150e506f8f71007a5ab8d55c364ec005f61729349" "q-252524/selection/0c27ba7b8e7e3262515046802ab5d7dda4fc4a2ee0901fa0b1772b0b9d67bc24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-252524" :question/id #uuid "c19ac983-6286-56e7-bf4f-85d61c5577fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-252524" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-252524/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-252524"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-237230/field-1/fc624ccef10fa5da761a2ced9283d1bb87ba02c1eb3f7973d693cdde9d86337f" :answer/id #uuid "6a1e4813-3faa-51f3-a6c7-5f380a270cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237230/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f1759f-0b67-5953-a229-7ecd8422ffd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-237230/field-1/fc624ccef10fa5da761a2ced9283d1bb87ba02c1eb3f7973d693cdde9d86337f" :answer-field/choices ["q-237230/field-1/fc624ccef10fa5da761a2ced9283d1bb87ba02c1eb3f7973d693cdde9d86337f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-237230" :question/id #uuid "62a37dcc-7584-5c66-9685-db6471626299" :question/math-academy-id "q-237230" :question/is-example false :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-237230/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237230"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection/ad889f734dcc6157c58365a943019216a8a06e57496d66f5613ab07508643e71" :answer/id #uuid "703bd43f-1439-51e0-8b47-3575c00f6846" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection/4654150ef28618566138956bf44fae030f6bc173d14f9ce88a58bcf45aa2dc8f" :answer/id #uuid "58c1935e-ff78-5933-b4e4-51a93992c003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection/76635a9a86d1ebce059613278c830f641a6ef6ed8f57237808b422b262d6ac90" :answer/id #uuid "18ba6f8c-015d-54f0-9a37-e5112a5a46b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection/37a3e830d14f899c170d86471a5cc9e75cd56ecad3439c9f3af76b6ae8fba015" :answer/id #uuid "b322489e-e53b-5226-af85-c6d93ba9eb20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection/9a3cc180cf3e0e0d9371d9ac5178119c6ca69902c548ba515ff999d6f8d9870c" :answer/id #uuid "deca52ed-b51e-5fd8-b96b-ecd8190eb94f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8569deb-6cf7-5d5c-84ef-2474c64086d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-23631/selection/ad889f734dcc6157c58365a943019216a8a06e57496d66f5613ab07508643e71" :answer-field/choices ["q-23631/selection/ad889f734dcc6157c58365a943019216a8a06e57496d66f5613ab07508643e71" "q-23631/selection/4654150ef28618566138956bf44fae030f6bc173d14f9ce88a58bcf45aa2dc8f" "q-23631/selection/76635a9a86d1ebce059613278c830f641a6ef6ed8f57237808b422b262d6ac90" "q-23631/selection/37a3e830d14f899c170d86471a5cc9e75cd56ecad3439c9f3af76b6ae8fba015" "q-23631/selection/9a3cc180cf3e0e0d9371d9ac5178119c6ca69902c548ba515ff999d6f8d9870c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-23631" :question/id #uuid "ebba1590-9e65-500b-8c4b-a4d7ba7a650f" :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-23631/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-23631"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-237231/field-1/c91fd6aa6d32305320a5e63a565d85b363f2f0eaf615bf0e25931140465f9773" :answer/id #uuid "fdc38579-e69e-53f0-b1d2-d53f0ff0043c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-237231/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6bd0d56-e288-5c4a-9f2c-1d0c00ef8147" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-237231/field-1/c91fd6aa6d32305320a5e63a565d85b363f2f0eaf615bf0e25931140465f9773" :answer-field/choices ["q-237231/field-1/c91fd6aa6d32305320a5e63a565d85b363f2f0eaf615bf0e25931140465f9773"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-237231" :question/id #uuid "f783ab8b-742a-5e04-8610-0016de741818" :question/math-academy-id "q-237231" :question/is-example false :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-237231/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237231"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
